WEBVTT

00:00:00.046 --> 00:00:01.776
A bookshop has one evening to bring

00:00:01.834 --> 00:00:05.050
different tastes together. Its readers, listeners and

00:00:05.108 --> 00:00:06.769
film regulars need a reason to join

00:00:06.815 --> 00:00:09.300
the same conversation. That is the job

00:00:09.346 --> 00:00:11.633
of Common Room. I start with public

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.885
cultural anchors for two or three circles.

00:00:14.512 --> 00:00:17.008
The venue, timing and minimum are editable.

00:00:17.299 --> 00:00:19.679
A hard veto means exactly that: the

00:00:19.737 --> 00:00:22.256
agent cannot quietly put the excluded work

00:00:22.349 --> 00:00:24.845
back in. This is the real public

00:00:24.903 --> 00:00:25.716
application.

00:00:26.447 --> 00:00:29.303
Common Room asks Qloo for cultural candidates,

00:00:29.489 --> 00:00:31.614
then compares the same shortlist for every

00:00:31.672 --> 00:00:35.770
circle. The stages follow actual execution. Waiting

00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:37.477
periods are shortened in this edit.

00:00:38.208 --> 00:00:40.588
Here is the program: one book, one

00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:43.189
artist and one film. The connecting question

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.906
gives the host a conversational arc. The

00:00:45.964 --> 00:00:48.576
entry points explain which selections rank strongest

00:00:48.622 --> 00:00:49.563
for each circle.

00:00:50.294 --> 00:00:52.999
These are relative ranks, not predictions about

00:00:53.046 --> 00:00:54.927
people, and the app does not invent

00:00:54.985 --> 00:00:56.053
a shared theme.

00:00:56.796 --> 00:00:58.805
The least-served circle comes first in the

00:00:58.851 --> 00:01:01.127
decision. Open the alternative to see what

00:01:01.185 --> 00:01:03.809
optimizing only the average would choose, and

00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:07.071
how each circle's coverage changes. Sometimes both

00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:08.917
policies choose the same program.

00:01:09.764 --> 00:01:11.215
Common Room shows that too.

00:01:12.226 --> 00:01:14.536
Suppose the host cannot use this artist.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.602
I add the exact identity as a

00:01:16.649 --> 00:01:19.575
veto, review the changed brief, and compare

00:01:19.609 --> 00:01:22.698
again. The new result confirms the exclusion

00:01:22.756 --> 00:01:24.474
from the actual discovery evidence.

00:01:25.159 --> 00:01:27.063
If the minimum is too strict, the

00:01:27.121 --> 00:01:30.082
agent makes one targeted refinement. When the

00:01:30.128 --> 00:01:33.182
evidence still falls short, it stops. The

00:01:33.251 --> 00:01:36.618
rejected selections stay diagnostic. No approved host

00:01:36.653 --> 00:01:37.710
program is produced.

00:01:38.569 --> 00:01:40.485
For a feasible night, the host gets

00:01:40.543 --> 00:01:43.236
preparation notes, a timed running order and

00:01:43.306 --> 00:01:45.872
prompts tied to the selected works. The

00:01:45.918 --> 00:01:48.844
printable copy and downloads retain the evidence

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:52.083
and constraints. The host still checks availability,

00:01:52.118 --> 00:01:53.825
permissions and venue arrangements.

00:01:54.673 --> 00:01:57.053
Common Room supplies a grounded starting point

00:01:57.099 --> 00:01:59.026
for a shared cultural evening, with the

00:01:59.061 --> 00:02:00.710
tradeoffs open to inspection.
